[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186105723 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" "question-580b167f-860a-55e7-8509-7c52af963b20"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105580 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 4}\\log_2 (x+4) .$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4,$ so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(8) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105724 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-13360da3-e828-58d3-a8ca-a868355e39a5" "question-c386627f-74f0-5d5a-9a11-2fd0a0613fa9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105581 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y =  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right).$\n\n![](images/f0/f00e550e8b543f64effcbafb43aed9e43a4363a0c3060da4fcc23d18f76a872f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right) = \\infty.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105726 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-20f5e3df-f068-575a-bd88-8cc274ff8c19" "question-94ed6f2f-e6d7-5eca-a61d-41902b4a3cb4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105583 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x+2).$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105725 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-29481c76-b7fa-575a-9ace-40d060b32844" "question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105582 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x-2).$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" :question/id #uuid "d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-339829" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}$ is well defined for $x>-4,$ and $x\\ne 2$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=1$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(1)+8)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52a6db62-005f-550b-8c07-96f59d9e4b2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52a6db62-005f-550b-8c07-96f59d9e4b2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "52a6db62-005f-550b-8c07-96f59d9e4b2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer/id #uuid "e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-580b167f-860a-55e7-8509-7c52af963b20" :question/id #uuid "580b167f-860a-55e7-8509-7c52af963b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-54470" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{2}⁡(4x)$ is well defined for $x>0,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=4$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5806411-08d6-5ddd-b363-c73129ba0cfe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5806411-08d6-5ddd-b363-c73129ba0cfe" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5806411-08d6-5ddd-b363-c73129ba0cfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8c90079e-1664-5315-a4e8-b23619bb6336" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1c2ea8b-9131-5dd2-9555-9cdde1b9d662" "answer-2446d45d-0402-58a6-91cf-846d84f2cdb5" "answer-8c90079e-1664-5315-a4e8-b23619bb6336" "answer-cf438cbb-e1b4-525e-bb7c-32d71029f2b6" "answer-36fddc55-77af-52ed-88ba-e95230bef581"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1c2ea8b-9131-5dd2-9555-9cdde1b9d662" :answer/id #uuid "a1c2ea8b-9131-5dd2-9555-9cdde1b9d662" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2446d45d-0402-58a6-91cf-846d84f2cdb5" :answer/id #uuid "2446d45d-0402-58a6-91cf-846d84f2cdb5" :answer/type :answer.t