625d-0eac-56fb-9026-80a2e5480905"] ["question-29481c76-b7fa-575a-9ace-40d060b32844" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-ef353a81-f085-5f7c-bea2-edd66e33a6a2"] [17592186105725 :knowledge-point/questions "question-29481c76-b7fa-575a-9ace-40d060b32844"] ["question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b" :question/id #uuid "1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b"] ["question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b" :question/math-academy-id "q-67658"] ["question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$"] ["question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$\n\n![](images/5c/5c4b062938cc4e3d64f11f9ad3bd8da9431581e9a1b0278a977a64c0d727c73d.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] ["question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d"] ["answer-ce81e711-5293-5867-b8ca-b0091a8e51f5" :answer/id #uuid "ce81e711-5293-5867-b8ca-b0091a8e51f5"] ["answer-ce81e711-5293-5867-b8ca-b0091a8e51f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ce81e711-5293-5867-b8ca-b0091a8e51f5" :answer/value "-2"] ["answer-784225b7-fdbd-5ed5-98bf-a5c7fd5f5031" :answer/id #uuid "784225b7-fdbd-5ed5-98bf-a5c7fd5f5031"] ["answer-784225b7-fdbd-5ed5-98bf-a5c7fd5f5031" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-784225b7-fdbd-5ed5-98bf-a5c7fd5f5031" :answer/value "-\\infty "] ["answer-dd67f345-0127-58ed-a1c4-1ae6514154f5" :answer/id #uuid "dd67f345-0127-58ed-a1c4-1ae6514154f5"] ["answer-dd67f345-0127-58ed-a1c4-1ae6514154f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-dd67f345-0127-58ed-a1c4-1ae6514154f5" :answer/value "0"] ["answer-f2db4690-93ae-558e-9493-a372055b41d7" :answer/id #uuid "f2db4690-93ae-558e-9493-a372055b41d7"] ["answer-f2db4690-93ae-558e-9493-a372055b41d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f2db4690-93ae-558e-9493-a372055b41d7" :answer/value "\\infty "] ["answer-522c42bc-ffdf-5445-a281-9e67e13da065" :answer/id #uuid "522c42bc-ffdf-5445-a281-9e67e13da065"] ["answer-522c42bc-ffdf-5445-a281-9e67e13da065" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-522c42bc-ffdf-5445-a281-9e67e13da065" :answer/value "\\text{DNE}"] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/correct "answer-522c42bc-ffdf-5445-a281-9e67e13da065"] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/choices "answer-ce81e711-5293-5867-b8ca-b0091a8e51f5"] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/choices "answer-784225b7-fdbd-5ed5-98bf-a5c7fd5f5031"] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/choices "answer-dd67f345-0127-58ed-a1c4-1ae6514154f5"] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/choices "answer-f2db4690-93ae-558e-9493-a372055b41d7"] ["answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d" :answer-field/choices "answer-522c42bc-ffdf-5445-a281-9e67e13da065"] ["question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-3e1c1c73-b29d-551a-9e59-e04e1561113d"] [17592186105725 :knowledge-point/questions "question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b"] [17592186107069 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ below.\n\n![](images/00/004265d2269924c6ce8905b6b92e9050b84066b3b5a3cc5383c75dd08e62f413.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to3}\\log_2{x} = \\log_2{3}\\approx 1.58.\n$$\n\nFor logarithmic functions, the limit at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, so for any base $b \\gt 1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\log_b{x} = \\log_b{c}.\n$$\n\nRemember that logarithms are only defined for positive inputs $x\\gt 0,$ so we must have $c>0.$ Otherwise, the limit does not exist $\\textrm{(DNE}).$"] [17592186107070 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look again at the function $y=\\log_2(x).$\n\n![](images/30/30a66b56ea1422fc61941d22aedbbf3f17e06810e66e6590490bd4b7188fd361.png)\n\nAs $x$ gets larger and larger, the curve increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\log_2{x}= \\infty.\n$$\n\nThe curve is not defined for $x\\leq 0,$ but as $x$ approaches zero from the right the curve *decreases* without bound. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\log_2{x} = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE},\\qquad\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to0}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nThese properties are true for logarithms of any base $b\\gt 1.$"] [17592186106004 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0]] "retractions" [[17592186105580 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 4}\\log_2 (x+4)$."] [17592186105580 :question/worked-solution "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4$, so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4)\\log_{2} (x + 4) &= \\log_{2} (4 + 4) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (8) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (2^{3}) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105581 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)}$."] [17592186105581 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/f0/f00e550e8b543f64effcbafb43aed9e43a4363a0c3060da4fcc23d18f76a872f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right) = \\infty\n$$"] [17592186105583 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2)$."] [17592186105583 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+2)\n$$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq -2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty\n$$"] [17592186105582 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2)$."] [17592186105582 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x-2)\n$$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186107069 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ below.\n\n![](images/00/004265d2269924c6ce8905b6b92e9050b84066b3b5a3cc5383c75dd08e62f413.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to3}\\log_2{x} = \\log_2{3}\\approx 1.58\n$$\n\nFor logarithmic functions, the limit at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, so for any base $b \\gt 1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\log_b{x} = \\log_b{c}\n$$\n\nRemember that logarithms are only defined for positive inputs $x\\gt 0$, so we must have $c>0$. Otherwise, the limit does not exist $\\textrm{(DNE})$."] [17592186107070 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look again at the function\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2(x)\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30a66b56ea1422fc61941d22aedbbf3f17e06810e66e6590490bd4b7188fd361.png)\n\nAs $x$ gets larger and larger, the curve increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\log_2{x}= \\infty\n$$\n\nThe curve is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq 0\n$$\n\nbut as $x$ approaches zero from the right the curve *decreases* without bound. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\log_2{x} = -\\infty\n$$\n\nAlso, note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE},\\qquad\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to0}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nThese properties are true for logarithms of any base $b\\gt 1$."]]}]